# Intent: the robot knows when it is on its side or upside down (owner request 2026-10-21). (1) hal_imu.cpp now also tracks which way gravity points: a low-passed vector, the dominant axis (+x -x +y -y +z -z) when the pull is at least 6.5 m/s^2 and the total is between 0.8 and 1.2 g, a new axis only when it beats the old one by 2 m/s^2 and holds for about a second. Each change, and once a minute while it stays, pushes an "orientation" event {axis, gx, gy, gz in milli-g}; the host maps axes to poses so a wrong guess about the sensor mounting is fixed without a flash. (2) New MCP tool self.screen.set_rotation {degrees 0|180} flips the panel (mirror x and y) so an upside-down unit shows its face right-way-up; a 180 lasts 150 s unless sent again (the host re-sends every minute) and the IMU task puts it back to 0 when the lease ends, so a screen can never stay flipped when the host goes away; a boot always starts at 0.
# Upstream: m5stack/StackChan firmware/main/hal/hal_imu.cpp, hal_mcp.cpp, board/stackchan.cc, board/hal_bridge.h (MIT). Applies after 066.
# Check after a flash: hold the unit upright, on its left side, right side, upside down, face down and on its back for 3 s each: the host log shows "unit orientation: orientation axis=... gx=... gy=... gz=..." for each, and "StackChan orientation: <pose>" once the axis table is right; upside down flips the screen and returns it when set upright again.
